---
name: js-int-division-by-zero
description: Int division/remainder by zero FAILS on every lane instead of yielding JS Infinity/NaN
backends: [int, jvm, js]
---

# Integer division by zero must fail, not produce `Infinity`

`ssc` `Int` is 64-bit **integer** arithmetic, so `10 / 0` has no value — it must fail the way it
does on the interpreter and the JVM. The v1 JS backend used to emit `Math.trunc(a / b)`, so a zero
divisor produced JS's `Infinity` (and `NaN` for `%`): a silent wrong answer that no `try/catch` ever
saw, because nothing was thrown (`BUGS.md` `js-int-division-by-zero-yields-infinity`).

The cases below all *catch*, so the observable is the caught value rather than a crash — a program
that merely died would not show which lane produced what. Double division is included on purpose:
`4.0 / 0` is legitimately `Infinity` in Scala too, and pinning that here is what stops a fix from
over-reaching into floating point.

```scalascript
def divide(a: Int, b: Int): String =
  try { (a / b).toString } catch { case _ => "div-failed" }

def remainder(a: Int, b: Int): String =
  try { (a % b).toString } catch { case _ => "mod-failed" }

println(divide(10, 0))
println(remainder(10, 0))
println(divide(10, 3))
println(remainder(10, 3))

// Floating point keeps IEEE semantics — this must NOT become an error.
val f: Double = 4.0
println((f / 0.0).toString)
```
